```ts
function add(a: number, b: number): number {
  return a + b;
}
```

The original JS also concatenates strings, so `add("a", "b")` used to work. If you relied on that, use overloads instead of a loose union:

```ts
function add(a: number, b: number): number;
function add(a: string, b: string): string;
function add(a: any, b: any) {
  return a + b;
}
```

The first version is the one you almost certainly want, since an `add` that quietly glues strings together is a bug waiting for its moment.

This assumes `add` is exported from `add.ts` (`export function add...`). Jest can't test a function it can't import.

```ts
// add.test.ts
import { add } from "./add";

describe("add", () => {
  it("adds two positive numbers", () => {
    expect(add(2, 3)).toBe(5);
  });

  it("handles negatives and zero", () => {
    expect(add(-4, 1)).toBe(-3);
    expect(add(0, 0)).toBe(0);
  });

  it("handles floating-point values", () => {
    expect(add(0.1, 0.2)).toBeCloseTo(0.3);
  });

  it("propagates NaN and Infinity", () => {
    expect(add(NaN, 1)).toBeNaN();
    expect(add(Infinity, 1)).toBe(Infinity);
  });
});
```

That `toBeCloseTo` isn't optional: `0.1 + 0.2` is `0.30000000000000004`, and `toBe(0.3)` would fail.

If you went with the overloaded version, add this case:

```ts
it("concatenates strings", () => {
  expect(add("foo", "bar")).toBe("foobar");
});
```

